------------------------------------------- Pony component ------------------------------------------

- ADD A NICE PONY COMPONENT, WITH A BEAUTIFUL 8-BIT ARTWORK IMAGE.

- Instruction: Angular Prerequisite "Building components" and "Reacting to signal changes" chapters

1️⃣As we did for races, let’s create a component(Pony component) for ponies.
    🧠This component will have a ponyModel as a required input, which is the pony to display.
    The template must display the image of the pony, as well as its name in a figcaption tag,
    both of them in a figure tag.
    pony.html:

        <figure>
            <img src='image.jpg' alt='missing'/>
            <figcaption>Caption goes here</figcaption>
        </figure>
    
    🧠Create a computed signal in your component named ponyImageUrl(). It should return the full path
    of the image based on the color of the pony. For example, if the pony is PURPLE, the image URL
    should be images/pony-purple.gif.
    Hint: strings have a toLowerCase() method.


2️⃣The URL of the image must of course be dynamic, based on the color of the pony to display.
    Create a folder images in the public directory, where the public assets go, and copy the provided
    images inside public/images from resource5.

    🧠The template of the component should consist in a simple figure tag containing an img with a
    dynamic src attribute and will use a computed signal of our component named ponyImageUrl()
    to get it. 

    🧠You must also generate the alt attribute of the image and the figcaption text to be the pony’s
    name.
    pony.html:

        <figure>
            <img [src]="ponyImageUrl()" [alt]="ponyModel().name" />
            <figcaption>{{ ponyModel().name }}</figcaption>
        </figure>

    🧠Of course, you must implement computed signal from your component. It should return the full path 
    of the image based on the color of the pony. For example, if the pony is PURPLE, the image URL
    should be images/pony-purple.gif.


3️⃣Finally, the Race component should of course use this new component to display its ponies.


4️⃣Our component must also fire a custom event ponySelected when the user clicks on the element.

    🧠To achieve that, add a click listener on the root element of the template, calling a method of the
    component. This method will emit the pony using an output<PonyModel>() named ponySelected.
    For now, the parent component doesn’t listen to our custom event, but it will soon, don’t worry.


5️⃣To finish, let’s add a bit of style. In the file pony.css, add a CSS style for the figure and img
    elements containing the following rules:
    pony.css
    
        figure {
            margin: 3px;
            padding: 3px;
            font-size: 12px;
        }

        img {
            height: 50px;
        }


6️⃣Do not forget to copy all the test files and run ng test to be sure that everything is OK.